{
  "case_id": "2008-07-499",
  "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Do we begin with the assumption that this is a Chevron deference case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Your position is that any threat of serious injury is sufficient?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Being forced upon threat of death or serious injury to participate in the persecutory acts of his oppressors."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Your unprovable question upon unprovable question. I mean, to say that one question is really, really hard to figure out is no justification for laying on another one."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: I am quite curious why -- I am curious, why did you answer Justice Alito's question \"yes\"? I -- I would have thought that if you win this case, the Attorney General would still have tremendous leeway in deciding where, when, under what circumstances, the duress defense applied. Why isn't that so?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Mr. Pincus, refresh my recollection about something about the Fedorenko case, which I should have reread, but I honestly didn't in detail. Am I not correct that the point that Justice Alito makes was really part of the background of the case there was that nobody really believed his testimony? It was assumed, for purposes of decision, that he was telling the truth, but it was pretty clear he was not. Whereas here everybody does assume, for purposes of decision, that this man is telling the truth?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Mr. Pincus, I didn't -- I didn't get the bottom line of your exchange with Justice Breyer. Is it your position that there -- there has to be some -- some coercion defense, but it's up to the Attorney General to say what it is? I mean, he could say only if he were threatened with death?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What would be -- what would be the minimum. You say the statute is clear. It requires some culpability, how would you verbalize what is the lowest standard that the Attorney General could impose to read statute to infuse in it some element of culpability?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0001-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Serious bodily harm?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Katsas",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0001-0012",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. KATSAS: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: In categorical terms, the Immigration and Nationality Act provides that persons who assist or otherwise participate in persecution may not obtain certain immigration benefits."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But you do know that Aristotle was not construing this Federal statute, don't you?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In this case, Justice Scalia, our position is that knowledge is a sufficient mens rea and is clearly satisfied here where Petitioner, by his own testimony, knew about the mistreatment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "6eecd173282b39919a4a0086e23c8d35fbfdb621b68918c14d432c858adfad11",
      "evidence_id": "ev-6eecd173282b-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "(Whereupon, at 11:05 a.m., the case in the above-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2008-07-499",
